formatted_question:
According to the paper "Parameter-Efficient Orthogonal Finetuning via Factorization", in the definition of the butterfly factor $\bm{B}^F(k)$, the vectors $\bm{d}_i$ belong to
(A) \mathbb{R}^{\frac{k}{2}}
(B) \mathbb{R}^{\frac{d}{2}}
(C) \mathbb{R}^{b}
(D) \mathbb{R}^{2b}
(E) \mathbb{R}^{k}

raw_knowledge_statement:
Assuming that $k\geq 2$ is a power of $2$, we start by defining the \emph{butterfly factor} $\bm{B}^F(k)$ as

\begin{equation}\label{eq:but_factor}
\footnotesize
\begin{aligned}
    \bm{B}^F(k)=\begin{bmatrix}
    \text{diag}(\bm{d}_1) & \text{diag}(\bm{d}_2) \\
    \text{diag}(\bm{d}_3) & \text{diag}(\bm{d}_4)
    \end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{k\times k},
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

where $\bm{d}_i\in\mathbb{R}^{\frac{k}{2}},\forall i$ are some vectors.

Assuming that $k\geq 2$ is a power of $2$, we start by defining the \emph{butterfly factor} $\bm{B}^F(k)$ as

\begin{equation}\label{eq:but_factor}
\footnotesize
\begin{aligned}
    \bm{B}^F(k)=\begin{bmatrix}
    \text{diag}(\bm{d}_1) & \text{diag}(\bm{d}_2) \\
    \text{diag}(\bm{d}_3) & \text{diag}(\bm{d}_4)
    \end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{k\times k},
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

where $\bm{d}_i\in\mathbb{R}^{\frac{k}{2}},\forall i$ are some vectors.

formatted_question:
According to the paper "Parameter-Efficient Orthogonal Finetuning via Factorization", when the authors define the butterfly factor $\bm{B}^F(k)$ for $k\geq 2$ as a block matrix built from diagonal blocks, the ambient space of $\bm{B}^F(k)$ is
(A) \mathbb{R}^{\frac{k}{2}\times \frac{k}{2}}
(B) \mathbb{R}^{k\times k}
(C) \mathbb{R}^{d\times d}
(D) \mathbb{C}^{k\times k}
(E) \mathbb{R}^{2k\times 2k}

raw_knowledge_statement:
Assuming that $k\geq 2$ is a power of $2$, we start by defining the \emph{butterfly factor} $\bm{B}^F(k)$ as

\begin{equation}\label{eq:but_factor}
\footnotesize
\begin{aligned}
    \bm{B}^F(k)=\begin{bmatrix}
    \text{diag}(\bm{d}_1) & \text{diag}(\bm{d}_2) \\
    \text{diag}(\bm{d}_3) & \text{diag}(\bm{d}_4)
    \end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{k\times k},
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

where $\bm{d}_i\in\mathbb{R}^{\frac{k}{2}},\forall i$ are some vectors.

With $d=2^N$, we then define the $d$-dimensional \emph{butterfly matrix} $\bm{B}(d)\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}$ recursively as

\begin{equation}
\footnotesize
\begin{aligned}
    \!\!\bm{B}(d)=\tilde{\bm{B}}(d,d)\!\cdot\!\begin{bmatrix}
    \bm{B}_1(\frac{d}{2})\!\!\!\!\! & \bm{0} \\
    \bm{0}\!\!\!\!\! &\bm{B}_2(\frac{d}{2})
    \end{bmatrix}= \tilde{\bm{B}}(d,d)\tilde{\bm{B}}(d,\frac{d}{2})\cdots\tilde{\bm{B}}(d,2),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

where $\bm{B}_1(\frac{d}{2})$ and $\bm{B}_2(\frac{d}{2})$ are two $\frac{d}{2}$-dimensional butterfly matrices.

formatted_question:
According to the paper "Parameter-Efficient Orthogonal Finetuning via Factorization", the authors define the butterfly component $\tilde{\bm{B}}(d,k)$ as a block-diagonal matrix of size $d\times d$ with block size $k$ as
(A) \text{diag}(\bm{B}^F_1(k),\cdots,\bm{B}^F_{d/k}(k))
(B) \begin{bmatrix}\bm{B}_1(\frac{d}{2}) & \bm{0} \\ \bm{0} & \bm{B}_2(\frac{d}{2})\end{bmatrix}
(C) \tilde{\bm{B}}(d,d)\tilde{\bm{B}}(d,\frac{d}{2})\cdots\tilde{\bm{B}}(d,2)
(D) \text{diag}(\bm{R}_1(b),\cdots,\bm{R}_{d/b}(b))
(E) \begin{bmatrix}\text{diag}(\bm{d}_1) & \text{diag}(\bm{d}_2) \\ \text{diag}(\bm{d}_3) & \text{diag}(\bm{d}_4)\end{bmatrix}

raw_knowledge_statement:
With $d=2^N$, we then define the $d$-dimensional \emph{butterfly matrix} $\bm{B}(d)\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}$ recursively as

\begin{equation}
\footnotesize
\begin{aligned}
    \!\!\bm{B}(d)=\tilde{\bm{B}}(d,d)\!\cdot\!\begin{bmatrix}
    \bm{B}_1(\frac{d}{2})\!\!\!\!\! & \bm{0} \\
    \bm{0}\!\!\!\!\! &\bm{B}_2(\frac{d}{2})
    \end{bmatrix}= \tilde{\bm{B}}(d,d)\tilde{\bm{B}}(d,\frac{d}{2})\cdots\tilde{\bm{B}}(d,2),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

where $\bm{B}_1(\frac{d}{2})$ and $\bm{B}_2(\frac{d}{2})$ are two $\frac{d}{2}$-dimensional butterfly matrices.

formatted_question:
According to the paper "Parameter-Efficient Orthogonal Finetuning via Factorization", the BOFT forward pass output $\bm{z}$ is expressed in terms of the orthogonal matrix $\bm{R}(m,b)$, the base weight matrix $\bm{W}^0$, and the input $\bm{x}$ as
(A) \big(\bm{R}(m,b)\cdot\bm{W}^0\big)\bm{x}
(B) \big(\bm{R}(m,b)\cdot\bm{W}^0\big)^\top\bm{x}
(C) \big(\sum_{i=1}^m \tilde{\bm{B}}^b_{(d,i)}\cdot\bm{W}^0\big)^\top\bm{x}
(D) \big(\bm{W}^0\cdot\bm{R}(m,b)\big)^\top\bm{x}
(E) \big(\bm{W}^0+\bm{A}\bm{B}\big)^\top\bm{x}

raw_knowledge_statement:
Combining pieces, the forward pass in BOFT is

\begin{equation}
    \footnotesize
    \begin{aligned}\nonumber
    \bm{z}=\big(\bm{R}(m,b)\cdot\bm{W}^0\big)^\top\bm{x},~~~~\text{s.t.}~\bigg{\{}\bm{R}(m,b)=\prod_{i=1}^m \tilde{\bm{B}}^b_{(d,i)}~~\&~~\underbrace{\big(\tilde{\bm{B}}^b_{(d,j)}\big)^\top\tilde{\bm{B}}^b_{(d,j)}=\tilde{\bm{B}}^b_{(d,j)}\big(\tilde{\bm{B}}^b_{(d,j)}\big)^\top\!=\bm{I}_d}_{\forall j\in [1, m]}\bigg{\}},
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}

where we denote $\tilde{\bm{B}}^b(d,2^{m - i+1})$ as $\tilde{\bm{B}}^b_{(d,i)}$ for simplicity, and $\bm{I}_d$ is an identity matrix of size $d$.

formatted_question:
According to the paper "Parameter-Efficient Orthogonal Finetuning via Factorization", in the BOFT forward pass, the orthogonal matrix $\bm{R}(m,b)$ is defined in terms of the butterfly components $\tilde{\bm{B}}^b_{(d,i)}$ as
(A) \prod_{i=1}^{\log d} \tilde{\bm{B}}^b_{(d,i)}
(B) \prod_{i=1}^m \left(\tilde{\bm{B}}^b_{(d,i)}\right)^\top
(C) \tilde{\bm{B}}(d,d)\tilde{\bm{B}}(d,\frac{d}{2})\cdots\tilde{\bm{B}}(d,2)
(D) \sum_{i=1}^m \tilde{\bm{B}}^b_{(d,i)}
(E) \prod_{i=1}^m \tilde{\bm{B}}^b_{(d,i)}

raw_knowledge_statement:
Combining pieces, the forward pass in BOFT is

\begin{equation}
    \footnotesize
    \begin{aligned}\nonumber
    \bm{z}=\big(\bm{R}(m,b)\cdot\bm{W}^0\big)^\top\bm{x},~~~~\text{s.t.}~\bigg{\{}\bm{R}(m,b)=\prod_{i=1}^m \tilde{\bm{B}}^b_{(d,i)}~~\&~~\underbrace{\big(\tilde{\bm{B}}^b_{(d,j)}\big)^\top\tilde{\bm{B}}^b_{(d,j)}=\tilde{\bm{B}}^b_{(d,j)}\big(\tilde{\bm{B}}^b_{(d,j)}\big)^\top\!=\bm{I}_d}_{\forall j\in [1, m]}\bigg{\}},
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}

where we denote $\tilde{\bm{B}}^b(d,2^{m - i+1})$ as $\tilde{\bm{B}}^b_{(d,i)}$ for simplicity, and $\bm{I}_d$ is an identity matrix of size $d$.

formatted_question:
According to the paper "Parameter-Efficient Orthogonal Finetuning via Factorization", the orthogonal matrix $\bm{R}(m,b)$, given that it is composed of a product of multiple orthogonal butterfly components, belongs to the space
(A) \mathbb{R}^{n\times n}
(B) \mathbb{R}^{d\times n}
(C) \mathbb{R}^{2b\times 2b}
(D) \mathbb{R}^{k\times k}
(E) \mathbb{R}^{d\times d}

According to the paper "Parameter-Efficient Orthogonal Finetuning via Factorization", in the discussion of orthogonality constraints and counting edges between adjacent levels, for a $d\times d$ orthogonal matrix with $d$ non-zero entries, those entries can only be
(A) 0 \text{ or } 1
(B) e^{i\theta}
(C) any non-zero real values
(D) \pm \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{d}}
(E) $\pm 1 $.
